  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The District of Columbia received $21.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Obligations are down 32.2% year over year, and awarding activity totaled 33,247 contracts with an average award size of $641K. Fiscal totals peaked at $36B in FY24 and have moved to $31.4B in FY25 and $21.3B in FY26 to date. Computer Systems Design Services leads industry buying at $3.1B, followed by Engineering Services at $2.6B and Other Computer Related Services at $2.5B. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency at $4.1B, with the Department of Transportation at $2.9B and the General Services Administration at $2.8B. BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC is the top vendor at $812M, with HARRIS CORPORATION at $711M and DELOITTE CONSULTING LLP at $639M. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did FY2026 to date compare with recent fiscal years?",
        "body": "FY2026 to date obligations are $21.3B, down from $31.4B in FY25 and below the FY24 high of $36B. Obligations are down 32.2% year over year, reflecting a material decrease in buying through the partial fiscal year. The award count in FY2026 to date is 33,247 with an average award size of $641K, showing continued program-level activity despite lower total dollars."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest agencies, industries, and vendors buying in DC?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads buying at $4.1B, followed by the Department of Transportation at $2.9B and the General Services Administration at $2.8B. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry at $3.1B, followed by Engineering Services at $2.6B and Other Computer Related Services at $2.5B. BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC is the largest vendor at $812M, with HARRIS CORPORATION at $711M and DELOITTE CONSULTING LLP at $639M."
      }
    ]
